# General Project Rules

## Stick to Rust idioms
Use idiomatic Rust: ownership, borrowing, lifetimes, enums, and pattern matching. Avoid unnecessary unsafe code unless absolutely required.

## Prefer functional
Prefer a functional coding style to an imperative coding style. Sometimes an imperative style can keep the code readable, but in the case that it doesn't, use the functional style.

## One module = one concern
Organize the project as separate crates and modules by concern (parser, frontend, analysis, eval, runtime, and so on). The crate map and the libraries each concern uses are in `docs/architecture/README.md`; prefer those libraries over adding new dependencies that do the same job.

## Prefer enums over trait objects
Use enums with variants instead of trait objects where performance and clarity are important (e.g., for AST nodes).

## Clear and simple error handling
Use `Result<T, E>` for all fallible operations. Define clear custom error types per module, and make every diagnostic convertible to `oneil_shared::error::OneilDiagnostic`. Follow `docs/CODING_STANDARDS.md` for recoverable vs. unrecoverable errors.

## Avoid premature optimization
Prioritize clarity and correctness in early stages; optimize only when there's a proven bottleneck.

## Comments
Write a documentation comment for every function. If the function is public-facing, ensure that the documentation comments include doc tests as well.

## Good tests
Write tests to test the code. This includes both unit tests and integration tests. Behaviour that spans phases belongs in `oneil_snapshot_tests`.

## Test Refactoring
When refactoring tests that don't compile, do not do anything more than getting them to compile unless explicitly directed. If a test is failing after it compiles, then the developer should be the one to figure out why.

## Editing
Any time you change code, or do a refactor, run `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features` and `cargo test --all-features` when the change is complete. The git hooks format and regenerate TypeScript bindings on commit, apply `clippy --fix` after commit, and refuse a push if clippy or tests fail.

## Checking out a worktree
Put worktrees in the `.worktrees` folder, and run `git config core.hooksPath .githooks` in each one so the hooks run while developing.

## PR Workflow
Work within a worktree unless explicitly asked to work in `~/careweather/oneil`. Before creating the worktree tell the developer what you are doing with a short explanation of worktree based development. When doing a large refactor and changing directions ask the user if they would like to stash the current progress in a local branch before switching directions. Keep the branch history clean: make a fixup commit for changes that belong to an earlier commit on the branch, and autosquash the fixups before pushing. Once a PR has review feedback, put the work that addresses it in a commit addressing the feedback. Fold unrelated new changes into the earlier commits and add a note to the PR about the change.

# Oneil Rules

Oneil is implemented in Rust in the `src/` workspace. `src/oneil` is the umbrella crate that re-exports the other crates and builds the CLI binary. Python is supported only as an interop target for model imports (`oneil_python`).

## Know the pipeline before changing it
Read `docs/architecture/compiler-pipeline.md` before changing the parser, frontend, analysis, evaluator, or runtime. It defines what each phase consumes and produces, what it validates, and what is fixed after it. Keep these invariants:
- Never mutate data from an earlier phase: ASTs, resolved templates, and cached unit graphs are only ever cloned.
- Runtime designs are applied only during composition.
- Graph-time diagnostics live in the `InstanceGraph` error buckets, never on individual `InstancedModel` nodes.
- Go through `oneil_runtime::Runtime` from the CLI and LSP; don't call phase crates directly from them.
Update that document when a phase's contract changes.

## Check as early as possible
Put every check and transformation in the earliest phase that has the information it needs, and only leave value-dependent rules in evaluation (see `docs/decisions/2026-11-20-check-as-early-as-possible.md`). Within a phase, fold independent per-node work into the existing traversal; add a sequential pass only when it needs a whole-graph result that an earlier pass produces.

## Keep a specification that is up-to-date
Ensure that there is a grammar and a specification that defines the language, and update it any time new things are added to the language. These documents live in `docs/specs/` (the grammar is `docs/specs/grammar.ebnf`). User-facing behaviour is documented in the guide under `docs/guide/`.

## Reference implementation description
Details about the Oneil programming language can be found in `README.md` on the top level and in the user guide under `docs/guide/src/`.

## Architectural Decision Records (ADRs)
Whenever a decision needs to be documented, make a new ADR in `docs/decisions` using `template.md`. The ADR file should be named as `<yyyy>-<mm>-<dd>-<adr-name>.md`. When a decision replaces an earlier one, update the earlier ADR's status to point at the new one.

An "ADR" may also be referred to as a "decision".

## Use recursive descent
The parser uses `nom` combinators (with `nom_locate` for spans) in a recursive-descent style, with no separate lexer. Keep new syntax in that style.

## AST should be expressive but minimal
Avoid unnecessary intermediate nodes; keep the tree clean and navigable.

## Wire types
Rust types sent to the editor or written as JSON reports are the source of truth. After changing them, run `./scripts/generate-ts-interfaces.sh`; never edit `packages/ts-interfaces/src/generated/` by hand.
